{"kind":"artifact","text":"Deployment note repaired at exact head 9529e444c5be20b29d87afaf247370bccf54345b, against round-15 report 3c473e14109543bf91ea2e601b53a614d88e6f59. Worktree clean, make vet and make test pass, whitespace clean.\n\nFinding 1 was right and it was right about the thing I had been most confident of. I reproduced your topology before touching a word: F parented R-first with L as a side parent is a legitimate fast-forward, a stock receiver takes it, and the first-parent walk is exactly F, R -- terminating at the known root while hiding every batch from L back. So the assertion is gone, and with it the argument that a second root \"cannot be named\", which was another cannot-happen shape.\n\nWhat replaces it is your other suggestion, the bounded side-parent rejection, and the measuring is what made it a mechanism rather than a hope. F needs L in its ancestry or the push is refused non-fast-forward, so every side-parent slot now has bounded ancestry: profile and candidates parentless, E on the sequence. Reachability of L then implies first-parent containment, which is the property the walk needed and Git's fast-forward test does not give. I also measured the two ways to break it -- hiding L behind a candidate-shaped commit restores both the fast-forward and the clean walk, and only the parentless-candidate rule rejects it; a gitlink in a candidate's tree does not restore it, which is the old claim-tree finding working in our favour for once.\n\nI did not close the whole thing and the note says so. A force update of the ledger branch leaves an internally consistent short branch with nothing in the graph to detect. That is the drain principal's door, already covered by \"a forge compromise is a key compromise\", and case 7 only ever proved a submitter cannot. Measured both reader defaults while writing it: a stock clone's forcing refspec adopts a rewind silently, dropping the plus rejects it, and that same non-forcing refspec still accepts the truncating fast-forward. Sequence commits have one parent, so rewind and truncation are one condition there; ledger entries are merges, so containment is a separate step no fetch setting supplies.\n\nFinding 2 is closed by giving each position a rule that ends at a trust anchor rather than at a shape, since \"type and content\" ruled out nothing -- measured, anyone's commit-tree output parses as an entry. Predecessor is the root by OID or a sequencer-signed entry; profile is the bundle governance has in force at base, resolved from the sequence rather than read from the entry; E is a signed sequence commit equal to head; candidates are parentless and their own signature need not verify, because unsigned is a verdict rather than a disqualification. Case 11 carries a commit-shaped impostor in each, plus the truncation topology moved out of case 4, where it never belonged since a submitter cannot write the ledger at all.\n\nFinding 3 I closed by narrowing the claim rather than by acquiring a closure. A version string is a name for something somebody else keeps, so \"an auditor holding that commit can execute it\" was false. Retaining an executable closure is recorded as available and not adopted; the note takes the external obligation and states it in the same terms as retention is a policy at the top of the file. Replay is now: bundle plus any interpreter for contract version V reproduces the verdict, bundle alone reproduces nothing and reports profile-unavailable rather than a fault, with both controls in case 4 -- the negative one fails if the auditor blames the drain and fails equally if it quietly runs today's interpreter.\n\nFinding 4 became a parser rule and a measurement. When the walk reaches the root OID it stops and takes base = head = the object named by the genesis-hex in the root's own message. Against the real object: 5d26227488 is a commit, parentless, the last entry of the sequence's first-parent walk over 673 commits, and it carries the descriptor a replay needs. The root is specified as bytes and hashed by an implementation that is not Git, matching commit-tree exactly.\n\nOur SHA-256 roots do not agree and I could not reproduce yours from any serialization variant. The note records the disagreement rather than resolving it, with the likeliest explanation named as a guess: a SHA-256 workroom's genesis-hex is a 64-character name, so we probably hashed different message bytes. It also stops quoting constants -- a root OID without its genesis and object format means nothing.\n\nThe consistency sweep found two more pairs, both mine. Candidates had to be parentless at intake as well as in the roles, or the halves disagree. And \"E is already reachable\" was true through an admitted entry and false for an all-refused one now that the root names the genesis rather than parenting it, so the note says which, and says retaining the sequence is the sequence ref's job.","body":{"branch":"task/deployment-story","commit":"9529e444c5be20b29d87afaf247370bccf54345b","head":"9529e444c5be20b29d87afaf247370bccf54345b","path":"notes/2026-08-07-deployment.md"}}